{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787080146,"nonce":"isnuVJR3sVtAeMRz4pe/3LTN2iY=","title":"`00036`'s table comments still say the thirty days is derived and that no API reads the raw tables, and both were made false by changes that shipped after it","message":"**What is wrong.** Two `COMMENT ON TABLE` statements in `db/migrations/00036_retention_defaults.sql` assert four things about the raw ingest tables. Two of the four are now false, and schema comments are the documentation `\\d+` hands whoever is holding a psql prompt at 03:00.\n\n`:94` on `ingest_batches` states: *\"THE 30 IS DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN (ADR 0024): it is the alert_event_keys idempotency horizon, past which SPEC acceptance criterion 36's replay-after-a-parser-fix would append the timeline a second time — so a payload kept longer cannot be used for the thing it is kept for. Move one number and you move both.\"* It closes with *\"Nothing a product surface renders is served from here; there is no API that reads this table.\"* `:97` on `ingest_rejections` states: *\"⚠️ No API reads this table yet, so the rejection feed this table exists to serve does not exist and the rows age out unseen.\"*\n\n**The demonstration.** The derivation is retracted at the top of its own ADR. `docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:15-18`: *\"⚠️ **The `raw_retention_days` derivation in the Decision below is RETIRED.** `oto replay` gates on supersession, not on age, so the 30 is a **chosen** number and its justification lives in Amendment 4. The number itself did not change.\"* Ticket `747bde8` is the retraction, and its comment `7e407eb` records that a tool written this session restated the same false claim and had to be corrected — so this is a sentence that actively propagates.\n\nBoth API claims are refuted by one file. `internal/sources/api/feeds.go:60` serves `GET /api/v1/sources/{id}/rejections` under a comment that reads *\"⭐ THIS IS THE SCREEN `ingest_rejections` EXISTS FOR … until this endpoint the only way to read one back was `psql`\"*; `:125` serves `GET /api/v1/sources/{id}/failed-batches`, *\"the batch-level half of the same question\"*, over `ingest_batches`. `internal/app/container.go:1042` wires both, and `internal/platform/tuning/defaults.go:156-157` already names them as *\"the DEPTH of two operator screens\"* — so the tuning copy knows what the migration comment denies. The `ingest_batches` comment is therefore false in the way that matters most for a retention setting: it tells the reader that shortening the window costs no product surface, when it shortens exactly how far back an operator can ask \"what did oto refuse, and why\".\n\n**Why it matters.** A retention window is the one setting whose blast radius is irreversible — deletion is `DROP PARTITION` — and these two comments are what a reader consults before moving it. One tells them the number is not theirs to choose (it is), and the other tells them nothing reads the data so the choice is free (two operator screens read it). Together they argue for lowering `raw_retention_days`, which is the one direction that silently deletes the evidence behind a 202. The `ingest_rejections` comment goes further and describes the rejection feed as unbuilt, which is a maintainer reading the schema and concluding there is work to do that is already done.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/00036_retention_defaults.sql:94` (`COMMENT ON TABLE ingest_batches`) and `:97` (`COMMENT ON TABLE ingest_rejections`) — the Down block repeats both at `:155` and `:158` · `docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:13-18` and Amendment 4 (the retraction) · `internal/sources/api/feeds.go:60`, `:125` (the two endpoints) · `internal/app/container.go:1042` (the wiring) · `internal/platform/tuning/defaults.go:156-157` (the copy that already knows).\n\n**Scope.** small. It is one new migration containing two `COMMENT ON TABLE` statements and nothing else.\n\n**Done when.** `00036` is not touched — this repo's rule is that an applied migration states the schema as it was at its own version and is not editable, which `00036:99` itself invokes for the `alert_events` rename. A new migration re-states both comments: `ingest_batches`'s says the thirty days is CHOSEN, points at ADR 0024 Amendment 4 for the justification, and names `GET /api/v1/sources/{id}/failed-batches` as a reader; `ingest_rejections`'s drops the \"no API reads this yet\" warning and names `GET /api/v1/sources/{id}/rejections` as the feed it was waiting for. Neither comment claims a derivation, and a reader deciding whether to lower `raw_retention_days` learns from the schema itself which screens go dark when they do.","files":null}]}